{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And the people stood by Moses from the morning until the evening.</b> From here we learn that one does not judge at night. And our Sages of blessed memory (Shabbat 10) said that any judge who judges a true judgment to its truth, even for a single hour, Scripture accounts it to him as though he became a partner to the Holy One, blessed be He, in the work of Creation, as it says, ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב וַיְהִי בֹקֶר״. And the matter requires explanation, for it appears that the mention of evening and morning in these two places are not alike — for here it begins from the morning and ends in the evening, while in the work of Creation it begins from the evening and ends in the morning. \nUnderstand and know that this world as a whole is sustained only when there is justice below or above, for the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to create the world with the attribute of judgment, and even though He joined to it also the attribute of mercy, nevertheless the attribute of judgment still stands in its place, for ״מֶלֶךְ בְּמִשְׁפָּט יַעֲמִיד אָרֶץ״ (Proverbs 29:4). And in the Midrash (Deuteronomy Rabbah 5:4) Rabbi Elazar said: In the place where there is justice, there is no justice; and in the place where there is no justice, there is justice. How so? If there is justice below, there is no justice above; if there is no justice below, there is justice above. And upon this order the world is established, that there is no hour free of justice, for it is known that the attribute of judgment above holds sway specifically at night. Even though the Holy One, blessed be He, renders judgment by day — that is, when there is no justice below by day — then the Holy One, blessed be He, also judges by day among those below for the violence that is among them. But if there is justice below by day, nevertheless there is also justice above among the heavenly princes. If so, the time of judgment above is from evening until morning, and the time of judgment below is from morning until evening. \nAnd for this reason the judge becomes a partner to the Holy One, blessed be He, in the work of Creation, and part for part they establish the foundations of all the worlds that stand upon judgment: for the King of the universe, through judgment, establishes earth and heaven from evening until morning, and the rabbinic kings, through judgment, establish the earth from morning until evening. And these matters are exalted and sound, and it is a precious thing." "<b>From the morning until the evening.</b> Rashi explained: Is it possible to say so? Rather, it comes to tell you, etc. — even a single hour, Scripture accounts it to him as though he were engaged in Torah the entire day. If so, what is this that Jethro said, ״וְכָל הָעָם נִצָּב עָלֶיךָ מִן בֹּקֶר עַד עָרֶב״? For in truth he was not judging from morning until evening, but Scripture merely accounts it to him as though he were judging, etc., and as though he became a partner in the work of the morning and the evening that are known by the instruction of the definite article ה׳ — an allusion to the work of Creation. And furthermore it is difficult, for the verse says ״וַיַּעֲמֹד הָעָם עַל מֹשֶׁה״ (Exodus 18:13), while Jethro said ״וְכָל הָעָם נִצָּב עָלֶיךָ״ (Exodus 18:14) — why did he use ״נִצָּב״ instead of the language of standing? And here the language of standing was put before ״the people,״ while Jethro put ״the people״ before the language of ״נִצָּב.״" "And it appears to me that the language of standing (עֲמִידָה) applies to those who stand before the judges before the conclusion of the trial, as it is written (Deuteronomy 19:17) ״וְעָמְדוּ שְׁנֵי הָאֲנָשִׁים אֲשֶׁר לָהֶם הָרִיב״. And the term ״נִצָּב״ applies after the conclusion of the trial, for their way was to make strife with the judge, as it is written (Deuteronomy 1:12) ״טָרְחֲכֶם וּמַשַּׂאֲכֶם וְרִיבְכֶם״. For the term ״נִצִים״ and ״נִצָּבִים״ denotes matters of strife and contention, and Dathan and Abiram prove it, as it is written (Isaiah 3:13) ״נִצָּב ה׳ לָרִיב וְעֹמֵד לָדִין עַמִּים״. \nAnd in every place where He mentioned Israel with the term ״the people,״ it is an epithet for the wicked, as Rashi explained on the verse ״וַיְהִי הָעָם כְּמִתְאֹנְנִים״ — ״the people means none other than the wicked,״ etc. (Numbers 11:1). And so here our Sages of blessed memory said (Avot 1:8) that when the litigants stand before you, they should be in your eyes as wicked. Therefore it says here, concerning the beginning of the trial, ״וַיַּעֲמֹד הָעָם״ — for it is possible that they are not wicked, but the standing for judgment caused them to be regarded as wicked; therefore the language of standing was put before ״the people.״ But Jethro speaks of those matters of strife that they made with Moses after the conclusion of the trial — for had they accepted it upon themselves, they would have been like the righteous; but because they picked a fight with the judge, he therefore called them wicked even after the conclusion of the trial. Therefore it says ״וְכָל הָעָם נִצָּב עָלֶיךָ״ — for on account of their being wicked they therefore rose up to contend, for every good man accepts the judgment upon himself and does not make strife with the judge; therefore he put ״the people״ before ״נִצָּב.״" "And according to this explanation, Jethro did not ask Moses about the length of time of the judgment lasting the entire day, for in truth he was not judging the entire day but only the time of judges, which is six hours. Rather, he asked about what he saw — that after the conclusion of the trial they rose up to contend with him from morning until evening, all day long crying out against him. And Jethro thought: perhaps Moses did them some thing on account of which they are contending with him. And it is written ״וַיַּרְא חֹתֵן מֹשֶׁה אֵת כָּל אֲשֶׁר הוּא עֹשֶׂה לָעָם״ (Exodus 18:14) — he looked and scrutinized this way and that at all the other things, to see whether he could find any thing that he was doing to the people improperly, and he searched and found nothing. \nAnd therefore he asked him ״וַיֹּאמֶר מָה הַדָּבָר הַזֶּה אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה עֹשֶׂה לָעָם״ (Exodus 18:14), for he wished to know whether he was doing any thing to the people besides the judgment itself. And after Moses did not answer him, he understood from this that he surely was doing nothing to them. And then Jethro reasoned: it is surely on account of the judgment itself that they are contending with him — and everyone found liable in his judgment rises up to contend with him. Therefore he asked him ״מַדּוּעַ אַתָּה יוֹשֵׁב לְבַדֶּךָ״ (Exodus 18:14), for if there were many judges with you, then the one held liable would not know who held him liable and against whom to cry out. And since you sit alone — for this reason all the people, that is, the lowly ones, are stationed upon you to contend from morning until evening."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:29.675229486Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}